message.content:
{
  "answer_markdown": "The expiration period of an invitation (API key) can be customized by an **Organization Admin** or **Workspace Admin** through the following rules:\n\n- The **maximum validity period** is set in days and applies only to **newly created API keys** [1].\n- If no expiration date is specified when creating a key, the system assigns the **maximum allowed expiration period** defined by the policy [3].\n- If an expiration date is set **beyond the maximum allowed**, the key is created with the **latest allowed expiration date** [3].\n- Existing keys are **not affected** by policy changes; they retain their original expiration date (if any) [2].\n\nTo configure the policy:\n1. **Organization Admins** can set the policy via [Admin Panel > API > API Keys](https://admin.mistral.ai/organization/api-keys) [1].\n2. **Workspace Admins** can set a Workspace-specific policy via [Admin Panel > Administration > Workspaces > API keys tab](https://admin.mistral.ai/organization/workspaces) [1].\n\nThe **default policy is off**, meaning API keys can be created without expiration unless explicitly enabled [1].",
  "citations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "API key expiration policy are **off by default**. An Organization Admin must enable a policy before key creation is restricted."
    },
    {
      "n": 2,
      "quote": "Existing keys are not affected. A policy applies only to newly created keys, so current integrations keep working unless you revoke a key or the key reaches its original expiration date."
    },
    {
      "n": 3,
      "quote": "If a policy is active, the key is created with the latest expiration date allowed by the policy. For example, if the maximum is 90 days, a key created without an expiration date expires in 90 days."
    }
  ],
  "insufficient_evidence": false
}

